    "notes": "Joseph Guarneri del Gesu, 1744, the \"Sainton, Betti\". The source publishes no dimensions at all. Body length is taken as 357 mm from the Kreisler del Gesu by way of the Goldberg, and the lower bout follows from the photograph's own length-to-width ratio of 1.7503 — which agrees with the Goldberg's HtoW to 0.03%, so the form is consistent with the other del Gesus here even though the absolute scale is inherited rather than measured. If the true body length is not 357, every length scales with it.\n\nBout widths, corner positions, bout radii and the corner structure come from fitting the recipe solver to the right-hand silhouette: 0.41 mm mean deviation. The precision floor matters more than that figure. The only image available is a 1080x764 sample page from a photo edition, about 1.55 px/mm against 10 px/mm for the museum-scanned entries in corpus/, and at that resolution the choice of segmentation threshold alone moves the plate length by a millimetre. Treat this outline as about a millimetre less certain than a corpus entry. Simple corners fitted best; the straight-run variant did not solve.\n\nIt sits in restricted/ rather than corpus/ because the reference image is a copyrighted commercial photo edition, not an open-licence museum record, and permission has not been obtained. Right side only. No arching, no f-holes."
  },
